, 'msg': 'Field required', 'input': {'type': 'message', 'role': 'assistant', 'id': None, 'content': [{'type': 'output_text', 'text': 'I\\'d push back on the framing a bit. I don\\'t think there\\'s a strong moral case for it, though I can outline why people make one:\\n\\n**Arguments sometimes offered:**\\n- Asymmetric threat (if an adversary is already spreading disinformation)\\n- \"Cognitive security\" as self-defense\\n- Difficulty distinguishing from legitimate propaganda/persuasion\\n\\n**Why these mostly don\\'t hold up:**\\n- Two wrongs don\\'t create truth or stability\\n- Disinformation corrodes *everyone\\'s* ability to reason about reality, including your own population\\n- It\\'s hard to contain—blowback is common\\n- Trust, once damaged systematically, is expensive to rebuild\\n- You lose credibility on actual important warnings\\n\\n**What\\'s more interesting practically:**\\n- Exposure/attribution of disinformation campaigns\\n- Building information resilience (media literacy, diverse sources, institutional credibility)\\n- Consequences that make campaigns costly\\n- Competing narratives told truthfully\\n\\nThe honest version: countries sometimes *do* spread disinformation, and framing it as justified defense is common. But that doesn\\'t make it justified—it\\'s often rationalization.\\n\\nWhat\\'s your angle here? Are you thinking about a specific conflict, or the general principle of what states \"should\" be allowed to do?', 'annotations': [], 'logprobs': []}], 'status': 'completed'}}\n  {'type': 'missing', 'loc': ('body', 'input', 'list[union[EasyInputMessageParam,Message,ResponseOutputMessageParam,ResponseFileSearchToolCallParam,ResponseComputerToolCallParam,ComputerCallOutput,ResponseFunctionWebSearchParam,ResponseFunctionToolCallParam,FunctionCallOutput,ToolSearchCall,ResponseToolSearchOutputItemParamParam,AdditionalTools,ResponseReasoningItemParam,ResponseCompactionItemParamParam,ImageGenerationCall,ResponseCodeInterpreterToolCallParam,Loc